epts and the slope coefficients are different\nfor all individual, or cross-section, units. This is to say that the investment\nfunctions of GE, GM, US, and WEST are all different. We can easily extend\nour LSDV model to take care of this situation. Reconsider (16.3.4). There we\nintroduced the individual dummies in an additivemanner. But in Chapter 9\n\nGujarati: Basic III. Topics in Econometrics 16. Panel Data Regression © The McGraw−Hill\nEconometrics, Fourth Models Companies, 2004\nEdition\nCHAPTER SIXTEEN: PANELDATAREGRESSION MODELS 645\non dummy variables, we showed how interactive,or differential, slope dum-\nmies,can account for differences in slope coefficients. To do this in the con-\ntext of the Grunfeld investment function, what we have to do is multiply\neach of the company dummies by each of the X variables [this will add six\nmore variables to (16.3.4)]. That is, we estimate the following model:\nY =α +α D +α D +α D +β X +β X +γ (D X )+γ (D X )\nit 1 2 2i 3 3i 4 4i 2 2it 3 3it 1 2i 2it 2 2i 3it\n+γ (D X )+γ (D X )+γ (D X )+γ (D X )+u (16.3.8)\n3 3i 2it 4 3i 3it 5 4i 2it 6 4i 3it it\nYouwillnoticethattheγ’sarethedifferentialslopecoefficients,justasα , α ,\n2 3\nandα arethedifferentialintercepts.Ifoneormoreoftheγ coefficientsare\n4\nstatisticallysignificant,itwilltellusthatoneormoreslopecoefficientsare\ndifferent from the base group. For example, say β and γ are statistically\n2 1\nsignificant.Inthiscase(β +γ )willgivethevalueoftheslopecoefficientof\n2 1\nX forGeneralMotors,suggestingthattheGMslopecoefficientofX isdif-\n2 2\nferentfromthatofGeneralElectric,whichisourcomparisoncompany.\nIf all the differential intercept and all the differential slope coefficients\nare statistically significant, we can conclude that the investment functions\nof General Motors, United States Steel, and Westinghouse are different\nfrom that of General Electric. If this is in fact the case, there may be little\npoint in estimating the pooled regression (16.3.1).\nLet us exam